Agent_A says:
    Simpson's paradox is a real statistical phenomenon, but it does not apply to the
    question as stated. The question asks whether a treatment can appear better in every
    subgroup yet worse overall when the groups are combined. If a treatment is better in
    every subgroup, then within each stratum the treatment outcome is superior. When you
    combine the groups, the overall result is a weighted average of those subgroup
    results. A weighted average of values that are all better than their counterparts
    cannot be worse than the overall control average, because every component favors the
    treatment. The apparent paradox arises only when the subgroup comparisons are made
    on different bases or when the aggregation weights differ between treatment and
    control in a way that reverses the direction, but that requires the treatment not to
    be better in every subgroup in the relevant sense. The tempting answer is "yes"
    because people recall Simpson's paradox, but Simpson's paradox involves a reversal
    where the treatment is better in each subgroup under one stratification and worse
    overall, which is possible only when the subgroups are not the same as the ones
    being combined or when the "better in every subgroup" claim is about unweighted
    proportions rather than actual subgroup-specific effects. Under a consistent
    definition of "better in every subgroup," the overall comparison must also favor the
    treatment. Therefore, the answer is no.
